# Explore Finite Do-Calculus as Logic

Begin with the distinction between [conditioning and intervention](https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/causal-models/). The first widget lets you assign target, observed, and intervened roles while enforcing the query language’s disjoint design.

{{interactive_demo:0}}

The number of legal expressions grows as $4^n$.

{{visualization:1}}

## From local rules to global equality

Three graphical side conditions create primitive links. Their reflexive, symmetric, and transitive closure is the full derivability relation.

{{visualization:2}}

{{interactive_demo:1}}

<details><summary>Click to reveal the canonical-model proof</summary>
A model is an equivalence relation containing every primitive link. Induction shows every finite derivation is valid in every model. Conversely, define the canonical model by declaring two queries equal exactly when a derivation connects them. Universal validity in this particular model implies derivability. Hence semantic validity and derivability coincide.
</details>

## Compute a certificate

The work-list algorithm finds a shortest rule path and stores predecessors.

{{algorithm:0}}

{{demo:0}}

{{visualization:0}}

## Why not inspect every model?

Universal Boolean-theory enumeration exactly mirrors the semantics and is ideal as a reference specification, but it examines up to $2^{N^2}$ tables for $N=4^n$ queries.

{{interactive_demo:2}}

{{algorithm:1}}

<details><summary>Click to reveal correctness of exhaustive identification</summary>
Every ordinary relational model has a Boolean characteristic table satisfying the theory checker, proving soundness. Every accepted Boolean theory defines an ordinary relational model, proving completeness. Combining this with the canonical-model theorem yields acceptance exactly when a derivation exists.
</details>

For repeated queries, precompute connected components with disjoint-set union.

{{algorithm:2}}

Further background on graphical separation is available in the [causal inference overview](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Causal_inference), while the self-contained paper in this package states the exact scope and all results proved by the finite relational theory.

